          %title
          %p
            I was until 2006 a professor of mathematics at the University of Wales Bangor (aka University of Bangor). The University closed down the Mathematics degree and I was ‘retired’! Since then I have been on various interesting visiting positions, whilst being Emeritus, until recently retaining an office in the Computer Science section of the university and continuing to work at least as hard as before (so I claim). I am also an emeritus member of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.wimcs.ac.uk/"} WIMCS
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://www.learnedsociety.wales/"} Learned Society of Wales
          .query
            %p
              Not offering a maths degree is one thing, but it seems that they don't even have a maths
              = succeed "!" do
                %em department
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby
            %p In the UK, there are several universities that do not have mathematics departments and in which engineers, computer scientists, physicists and chemists do not get any theoretically based mathematics to speak of. Biologists have traditionally received no mathematics training after the age of 16. (Sometimes it shows and is the bane of the statisticians detailed to teach biology students statistics, and don’t mention psychology students! Note also that quite a few of the so called ‘financial experts’ in the UK have little or no maths. Some do, and I am not saying that they were any better in the causes of the recent crisis!)
            %p
              In Bangor, it is even worse. Not only is there no mathematics department. There are very few mathematicians
              = succeed "," do
                %em per se
            %p
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim
            %p At least a couple of the top ten CS departments in the USA teach all of their own Maths classes. The actual Math department at most schools is too focused on calculus and analysis (at the undergraduate level). I think it has to be this way – it would be obscene for the CS department to dictate curriculum to the Math department.
            %p Don Knuth proposed that every professor should be required to have both a “major” and “minor” department, just like undergraduates must at most universities. That might be one step towards improving the situation – a way to influence the curriculum without compromising autonomy.
          %h2#what_you_can_find_here_and_on_my_personal_pages What you can find here and on my personal pages
          %p
            I have given a summary in my
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/timporter/show/HomePage"}
              private
              = succeed "Lab" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of some of the research area that I have worked in with references for further ‘enlightenment’. Some of these are in areas that continue to interest me, and I have various ‘projects’ and lists of ‘work in progress’ there as well, some of which are mentioned below. Some more recent ones have been added (August 2018).
          %p I am writing a series of notes on the theory and application of crossed gadgetry in algebra and topology, and some parts of these notes (approximately first 14 chapters) have been made available on the web at various times. I am currently trying to write new sections on the links between homotopy quantum field theory and non-Abelian cohomology.
          %p
            I am also very interested in directed homotopy theory and the application of ideas from the general area of the infinity category/homotopy toolkit in topological data analysis, artificial intelligence, and computer science. This relates both to the use of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+spaces"} stratified spaces
            and possibly to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            in TQFTs. Some material can be found on those personal pages or should be added .
          %p
            See also my
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/timporter/show/HomePage"}
                private
                = succeed "Lab" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Other goodies there include parts of a draft monograph on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/timporter/show/profinite+algebraic+homotopy"} profinite algebraic homotopy
          %p I have also put the start of a brief discussion of the relationships between the Cech and Vietoris methods and the newly developed methods of Topological Data Analysis.
          %h3#list_of_goodies List of Goodies!!
          %p
            Please go to
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/timporter/show/HomePage"}
              private
              = succeed "Lab" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            for more downloads and fuller details.
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Menagerie"} Menagerie
              \: a set of notes on crossed modules, higher dimensional analogues, simplicial techniques, homotopy coherence, and non-Abelian cohomology of various sorts. This version consists of 10 chapter. It will be updated periodically.
          %p
            Several ‘cut down’ versions of these notes have been prepared for various workshops and as they are less long may be of use. For instance in February, 2011, there was a Workshop and School on Higher Gauge Theory, TQFT and Quantum Gravity in the IST, Lisbon, (7-13 February, 2011). A set of notes was prepared using the Menagerie as a ‘mine’ from which to extract the sections relevant to the theme of the workshop, which were then edited slightly. The result was entitled:
            %strong Homotopy Quantum Field Theories meets the Crossed Menagerie: an introduction to HQFTs and their relationship with things simplicial and with lots of crossed gadgetry.
            A copy can be found
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/HQFT-XMenagerie.pdf", :title => ""} here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The ‘Luminy notes’: A cut down version of the Menagerie has been prepared for the LI2012 week on Algebra and computation (27 February – 2 March, 2012). A copy can be obtained by contacting me by e-mail or
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Rewriting-and-homotopy.pdf", :title => ""} here
            %li
              %p
                %em Abstract Homotopy Theory: The Interaction of Category Theory and Homotopy theory
                This article is an expanded version of notes for a series of lectures given at the
                %em Corso estivo Categorie e Topologia
                in 1991. They appeared in more or less this form in Cubo, 5 (2003) 115-165, 2003.
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒮
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
                  \-categories,
                  = succeed "-groupoids," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒮
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
                The notes were prepared for a series of talks that I gave in Hagen in late June and early July 2003, and, with some changes, in the University of La Laguna, the Canary Islands, in September, 2003.
            %li
              %p
                %em> Profinite Algebraic Homotopy
                \. There is a link to eight chapters of a draft monograph. Hopefully, this is likely to be published, so this is an incomplete version. (At present count the full version will probably have more than 1000 pages.) I have added new sections and even chapters to the earlier part, so the first 8 chapters of the current version do not correspond to these here.
            %li
              %p
                Grothendieck’s letters. Thirty years ago I was involved in a brief exchange of letters with Alexander Grothendieck. I will be putting up some excerpts. It may happen that commented versions of these will be made available by the
                %em Documents mathématiques
                project.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Spaces+as+infinity-groupoids.pdf", :title => ""}> Spaces as Infinity Groupoids
                \: this is a draft chapter of a (hopefully) forthcoming book,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em New Spaces in Mathematics and Physics
          %h2#more_information More Information
          %p
            You may also find me on:
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/users/3502/tim-porter"} MathOverflow
            |
            %a{:href => "http://orcid.org/0000-0001-7962-8471"} ORCiD
          %p and on Linkedin. (I have a gmail account and you can also contact me via that. The e-mail is initial\dot familyname\dot maths and then the normal ending for a gmail account.)
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/people"} people
